The `testing.locust.script.file` branch renders the file content with an extra leading space before the template action (and `nindent` already prefixes a newline). This typically produces a block scalar whose least-indented line is 1 space, causing the embedded Python locustfile to be indented and likely fail with `IndentationError`. Remove the leading space and render with the usual pattern `key: |\n{{ ... | nindent 4 }}` (no preceding literal whitespace).
```suggestion
{{- if .Values.testing.locust.script.file }}
  {{ .Values.testing.locust.script.key }}: |
{{ .Files.Get .Values.testing.locust.script.file | nindent 4 }}
{{- else }}
  {{ .Values.testing.locust.script.key }}: |
{{ .Values.testing.locust.script.inline | nindent 4 }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
```

The Make target assumes Helm release name == namespace (it uses `$(OCP_NS)` for both and execs `deploy/$(OCP_NS)-mcp-stack-locust`). That won’t work for common setups where release and namespace differ. Consider splitting into `OCP_NAMESPACE` and `OCP_RELEASE` (defaulting release to namespace) and using release for resource names.
```suggestion
OCP_NAMESPACE ?= $(OCP_NS)
OCP_RELEASE ?= $(OCP_NAMESPACE)
benchmark-ocp:                               ## Run MCP benchmark on OCP (requires OCP_NAMESPACE=<namespace> [OCP_RELEASE=<release>])
	@if [ -z "$(OCP_NAMESPACE)" ]; then echo "Usage: make benchmark-ocp OCP_NAMESPACE=<namespace> [OCP_RELEASE=<release>]"; exit 1; fi
	@echo "Starting MCP benchmark on OCP..."
	@echo "   Namespace: $(OCP_NAMESPACE)"
	@echo "   Release:   $(OCP_RELEASE)"
	@echo "   Config:    125 users, 30/s spawn, 60s"
	@oc -n $(OCP_NAMESPACE) exec deploy/$(OCP_RELEASE)-mcp-stack-locust -- \
		python3 -c "import urllib.request,urllib.parse; \
		urllib.request.urlopen(urllib.request.Request('http://localhost:8089/swarm', \
		urllib.parse.urlencode({'user_count':125,'spawn_rate':30,'run_time':'60s', \
		'host':'http://$(OCP_RELEASE)-mcp-stack-nginx'}).encode(), method='POST'))"
```
